1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,480
QLD and TQQQ follow the
Nasdaq-100 at 2x and 3x.

2
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:07,248
Saved for years, they seem
sure to leave far more,

3
00:00:07,248 --> 00:00:09,010
but how long can they last in a crash?

4
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:13,690
From 1999, let's compare 1x,
2x and 3x with a backtest.

5
00:00:14,690 --> 00:00:17,972
Like QLD and TQQQ, 2x
and 3x are calculated:

6
00:00:17,972 --> 00:00:21,030
2x or 3x the index each day, minus costs.

7
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:26,350
We buy $500 on the first trading
day of each month, with a 0.25% fee.

8
00:00:27,350 --> 00:00:28,940
Now let's roll the dates forward.

9
00:00:29,390 --> 00:00:33,451
The blue line is QQQ,
tracking the Nasdaq-100 at 1x;

10
00:00:33,451 --> 00:00:35,560
purple is 2x, orange is 3x.

11
00:00:36,010 --> 00:00:38,000
Yellow dotted line: money put in so far.

12
00:00:38,450 --> 00:00:40,850
Each step up the vertical
scale is 10 times.

13
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:44,410
All three accounts reinvest
the Nasdaq-100's dividends.

14
00:00:44,860 --> 00:00:50,030
At the end of 1999, the 3x account
was 3.28 times the $4,500 put in.

15
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:53,420
From March 2000, the dot-com
bubble starts to burst.

16
00:00:53,870 --> 00:01:00,680
On March 29, 2001, the 3x account
had lost 92.3% of the $12,000 put in.

17
00:01:01,130 --> 00:01:04,528
On April 4, 2x was down
80.7% and 1x 49.9%;

18
00:01:04,528 --> 00:01:07,530
all three were below the money put in.

19
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:11,190
Still, all three accounts
kept buying $500 every month.

20
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:15,480
In 2003, 3x rose 167.4%.

21
00:01:15,930 --> 00:01:18,480
At the end of 2007, all
three were nearly equal.

22
00:01:19,480 --> 00:01:21,400
Then came the 2008 financial crisis.

23
00:01:21,850 --> 00:01:27,610
On November 20, the 3x account had
lost 89.5% of the $58,000 put in.

24
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:33,890
On the same day, 2x had
lost 72.8% and 1x 36.4%.

25
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:37,870
In 2009, 3x rose 196.4%.

26
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:41,600
Until the end of 2012,
1x was still the largest,

27
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:44,060
but on July 3, 2013, 3x overtook 1x.

28
00:01:44,510 --> 00:01:47,980
In June 2017, the 3x
account passed $1,000,000.

29
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:56,190
In the COVID crash, 3x fell
69.7%, 2x 51.9% and 1x 28.5%.

30
00:01:56,640 --> 00:02:01,830
Still, the 3x account had about
$970,000, more than the $126,000 put in.

31
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:06,432
At the end of 2021, the 3x
account had about $9,100,000,

32
00:02:06,432 --> 00:02:08,360
widening to 8.66 times 1x.

33
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:11,540
In 2022 came a long decline.

34
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:15,159
The 3x account fell
from about $9,300,000 on

35
00:02:15,159 --> 00:02:18,400
January 3 to about
$1,800,000 on December 28.

36
00:02:18,850 --> 00:02:20,530
That's a loss of 80.9%.

37
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:25,460
2x fell 62.8% and 1x 34.4%.

38
00:02:25,910 --> 00:02:29,830
In 2023, 3x rose 198.2%.

39
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:34,120
In December 2024, the 3x
account passed $10,000,000.

40
00:02:34,570 --> 00:02:42,500
In the April 2025 plunge, 3x fell
56.8%, 2x 41.8% and 1x 22.7%.

41
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:46,220
October 15, 2026: the results.

42
00:02:46,670 --> 00:02:50,810
All three accounts put in about
$170,000 over 331 purchases.

43
00:02:51,260 --> 00:02:55,490
The 1x account is about $2,000,000,
and 2x about $7,900,000.

44
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:58,820
The 3x account was about $17,000,000.

45
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:05,190
The 3x account peaked on June
2, 2026, at about $20,000,000.

46
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:09,420
3x was 8.55 times 1x,
and 2x was 3.91 times.

47
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:13,620
Since July 3, 2013, 3x
has stayed above 1x.

48
00:03:14,070 --> 00:03:19,660
In the 14.3 years before that, 3x
was above 1x on only 10.8% of days.

49
00:03:20,110 --> 00:03:27,460
Days above the money put in were also
fewer for 3x: 1x 78.1%, 3x 64.7%.

50
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:31,020
Why did 3x shrink so much in crashes?

51
00:03:31,470 --> 00:03:35,150
Let's see what it means for a leveraged
ETF to reset its multiple every day.

52
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:40,010
Say the index rises 10% one
day and falls 10% the next.

53
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:42,730
1x loses 1%.

54
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:47,360
2x rises 20%, then falls 20%: a 4% loss.

55
00:03:47,810 --> 00:03:52,310
3x rises 30%, then falls 30%: a 9% loss.

56
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:56,200
The more swings repeat and the bigger
the multiple, the more is lost.

57
00:03:56,650 --> 00:04:00,410
Even if the index comes back, leveraged
accounts end up below where they started.

58
00:04:00,860 --> 00:04:05,550
In fact, in 2018, 1x
fell 0.1%, almost flat.

59
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,420
That year, 2x fell 8.6% and 3x 20.1%.

60
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:14,780
But what if you put it all in once
at the start, not every month?

61
00:04:15,230 --> 00:04:19,470
On April 1, 1999, each of the
three accounts got $10,000.

62
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:26,650
On March 9, 2009, the 3x account
had only $13 left: 99.87% was gone.

63
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:28,860
2x had $450.

64
00:04:29,310 --> 00:04:33,790
1x hit its low of $3,736
on October 9, 2002.

65
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:37,040
Here are the values on October 15, 2026.

66
00:04:37,490 --> 00:04:42,990
1x about $160,000, 2x about
$130,000, 3x about $18,000.

67
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:47,420
With a lump sum, 3x kept
only 11.4% of what 1x had.

68
00:04:47,870 --> 00:04:51,600
The monthly accounts kept buying,
even at prices cut by the crashes.

69
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:57,000
April 1999 was right before the dot-com
crash, so the start may have been bad.

70
00:04:57,450 --> 00:04:59,600
What if it had started in another month?

71
00:05:00,050 --> 00:05:02,434
We built 10-year monthly accounts,

72
00:05:02,434 --> 00:05:06,360
one starting each month from
April 1999 to October 2016.

73
00:05:06,810 --> 00:05:08,460
211 in total.

74
00:05:08,910 --> 00:05:13,590
After 10 years, 3x beat
1x in 175 of the 211.

75
00:05:14,040 --> 00:05:17,160
2x beat 1x in 183.

76
00:05:17,610 --> 00:05:22,730
Accounts left below the money
put in: 1x 4, 2x 16, 3x 20.

77
00:05:23,180 --> 00:05:26,860
The worst was the account
started in April 1999.

78
00:05:27,310 --> 00:05:33,040
Of about $61,000 put in, 3x
had about $12,000, 24.9% of 1x.

79
00:05:33,490 --> 00:05:38,690
The best was the account started in
September 2008, with 3x at 6.49 times 1x.

80
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:43,140
In all 31 of the 25-year
accounts, 3x came out on top.

81
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:48,000
We should also check how closely the
calculated 2x and 3x match real ETFs.

82
00:05:48,450 --> 00:05:50,770
This is how the 2x and 3x
accounts were calculated.

83
00:05:51,220 --> 00:05:57,260
The two ETFs launched in 2006 and 2010,
so from 1999 we have to calculate them.

84
00:05:57,710 --> 00:06:01,110
Each day, they follow 2x or 3x
the Nasdaq-100's daily return.

85
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:04,970
From that, we subtract a 0.95% fee
and interest on borrowed money.

86
00:06:05,420 --> 00:06:09,500
The interest is the US government's
three-month rate plus 1.02%.

87
00:06:09,950 --> 00:06:15,440
The 1.02% was set so the end value
matches the real QLD from June 2006.

88
00:06:15,890 --> 00:06:21,360
With the same value, TQQQ came out 0.57%
lower than the real one on a 1-year basis.

89
00:06:21,810 --> 00:06:23,820
Now let's look at the real ETFs.

90
00:06:24,270 --> 00:06:29,070
From March 2010, the month after
TQQQ launched, $500 every month.

91
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:31,200
The money put in is $100,000.

92
00:06:31,650 --> 00:06:36,210
QQQ grew to about $620,000,
and QLD to about $2,200,000.

93
00:06:36,660 --> 00:06:39,840
TQQQ was about $5,300,000.

94
00:06:40,290 --> 00:06:43,706
Over the same period, the
calculated 3x had about $5,100,000,

95
00:06:43,706 --> 00:06:45,330
a bit less than the real one.

96
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:53,150
The real TQQQ account also fell 81.5%
in 2022, and the QLD account 63.3%.

97
00:06:54,150 --> 00:06:55,270
Let's sum up.

98
00:06:55,720 --> 00:07:00,600
Buying monthly from 1999,
3x ended at 8.55 times 1x.

99
00:07:01,050 --> 00:07:05,430
But in 2001, there was a time when
92.3% of the money put in was gone.

100
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:08,590
In 2008, it was down 89.5% too.

101
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:12,840
It took 14.3 years for 3x to
stay ahead of 1x for good.

102
00:07:13,290 --> 00:07:17,920
With one lump sum at the start,
3x kept only 11.4% of what 1x had.

103
00:07:18,370 --> 00:07:21,382
Even in the 10-year accounts
with other start months,

104
00:07:21,382 --> 00:07:23,940
20 of the 211 left 3x
below the money put in.

105
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:27,090
The Nasdaq-100 rose a
lot over this period.

106
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:32,180
In the main test, the 2x and 3x
values are calculated, not real ETFs.

107
00:07:32,630 --> 00:07:34,790
If rates and fees change,
the results change too.

108
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:40,650
And to choose 3x, you had to endure losing
around 90% of the money put in, twice.

109
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:43,570
Taxes, currency and
inflation are excluded.

110
00:07:44,020 --> 00:07:47,310
It's not investment advice, but the
result of checking rules with code.

111
00:07:48,310 --> 00:07:52,080
Have a hypothesis to backtest
next? Tell us in the comments.

112
00:07:52,530 --> 00:07:54,080
Thanks for watching.
